The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018), and the relevant statements of its President,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Recalling the obligations of the Syrian Arab Republic under the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on Their Destruction and the relevant decisions of the Executive Council of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons,
Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons by anyone, anywhere and under any circumstances is unacceptable and constitutes a violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,
Expressing grave concern at the reports of chemical weapons use in Douma on 7 April 2018 and the resulting loss of life,
Stressing the need for a thorough, independent, impartial and objective investigation to establish the facts, and underlining that any attribution of responsibility must be based on sufficient, reliable and verifiable evidence,
Emphasizing that those responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable,
Reaffirming the need for all parties to comply with international humanitarian law and to allow safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access, including in accordance with resolution 2401 (2018),
Reaffirming that there can be no military solution to the Syrian conflict and recalling the importance of a political process in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported use of chemical weapons in Douma on 7 April 2018 and any use of chemical weapons anywhere, and expresses grave concern at the loss of life;
2. Calls for an immediate, independent, full and impartial investigation by the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission into the reports of chemical weapons use in Douma, and requests the Director-General of the OPCW to deploy the Fact-Finding Mission without delay, to collect and preserve relevant evidence, and to report to the Security Council within 30 days on the results of its investigation;
3. Calls upon all parties, including the Syrian authorities and all armed groups, to cooperate fully with the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission, to provide immediate, safe and unimpeded access to all relevant locations, persons and materials, and to ensure the safety and security of the Mission’s personnel;
4. Demands that all parties allow safe, immediate and unimpeded access for medical evacuations and humanitarian assistance to all people in need, in accordance with international humanitarian law and resolution 2401 (2018);
5. Calls upon all Member States to exercise maximum restraint and to refrain from any action that could further escalate tensions in the region or undermine the prospects for a peaceful settlement of the Syrian conflict;
6. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations;
7. Reaffirms that there can be no military solution to the Syrian conflict and calls for renewed efforts to implement resolution 2254 (2015);
8. Expresses its intention to consider the report of the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and any further measures, as appropriate, without delay;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days;
10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018), and the relevant statements of its President,
