1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported use of chemical weapons in Douma on 7 April 2018 and any use of chemical weapons anywhere, and expresses grave concern at the loss of life;
2. Calls for an immediate, independent, full and impartial investigation by the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission, in accordance with its mandate under the Chemical Weapons Convention and relevant decisions of the OPCW Executive Council, into the reports of chemical weapons use in Douma, and requests the Director-General of the OPCW to deploy the Fact-Finding Mission without delay, to collect and preserve relevant evidence, and to report to the Security Council within 30 days on the results of its investigation, including any impediments to the Mission’s access and cooperation by the parties, and, as appropriate, initial findings within 14 days; emphasizes that the Mission should operate with full respect for the sovereignty of the Syrian Arab Republic and that its findings should not be prejudged by any Member State;
3. Calls upon all parties, in particular the Syrian authorities and all armed groups, to cooperate fully with the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission, to provide immediate, safe and unimpeded access to all relevant locations, persons and materials, without preconditions, delay or obstruction, including by allowing the Mission to determine the locations, persons and materials it needs to examine, and to ensure the safety and security of the Mission’s personnel and witnesses;
4. Demands that all parties allow safe, immediate, sustained and unimpeded access for medical evacuations and humanitarian assistance to all people in need, including in Douma and all besieged and hard-to-reach areas, in accordance with international humanitarian law and resolution 2401 (2018), and calls for the full implementation of resolution 2401 (2018), including cessation of hostilities and humanitarian pauses, to enable the safe, unimpeded and sustained delivery of humanitarian aid and services and to ensure the protection of medical personnel and facilities;
5. Calls upon all Member States and all parties in Syria to exercise maximum restraint, to refrain from the threat or use of force and from any unilateral action, including military action, that could further escalate tensions in the region or undermine the prospects for a peaceful settlement of the Syrian conflict, to respect the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to act in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations;
6. Reaffirms its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations;
7. Reaffirms that there can be no military solution to the Syrian conflict and calls for renewed efforts to implement resolution 2254 (2015) under the auspices of the United Nations and in line with the Geneva process, including through the Astana and Geneva processes, and expresses its support for the efforts of the Special Envoy;
8. Expresses its intention to consider the report of the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and to consider, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, any further action only after the report has been carefully examined by the Council;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days;
10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

